{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR-RATIFIED DECISION (2026-08-04): FAIL CLOSED\n\nThe operator has ruled on the open design question from the prior RCA (the third\ndefect — the epic-close bug screen converting a systematic provider failure into\nper-candidate \"unrelated\" verdicts, silently blinding the caused_by bug floor):\n\n**An LLM provider error during the completion verifier must BLOCK the close** —\nnonzero exit, a clear error naming the provider failure, ticket stays\n`in_progress` — consistent with the absent-provider path, which already fails\nclosed. The escape hatch is the existing force flag (`--force-close` today;\nstory 24f7-1955-bcd8-4f29 separately renames it to `--force` — the rename is NOT\npart of this change).\n\nConcretely for this ticket: `LLMUnavailableError` raised anywhere inside the\ncompletion-verifier run — including from the epic-close bug screen's\nper-candidate calls — must propagate to the close gate's existing fail-closed\nhandler instead of degrading to a `C` (\"unrelated\") verdict. Non-provider\nper-candidate failures (malformed output, store read glitches) keep the\ndocumented degrade-open behavior: only the SYSTEMIC provider-error class fails\nclosed.\n\nImplementation proceeds under this ticket per the plan now recorded in the\ndescription."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785863004996009001,"uuid":"a118908c-2010-442d-a6a2-2de93c4a676a"}